## Why This Is Asked

Tests DP with a non-adjacent removal constraint and requires reconstruction of which indices were removed.

## Key Concepts

- 3D DP state dp[i][t][r]
- Non-adjacent constraint during removal
- Path reconstruction for the chosen indices

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxRemainingSum(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = Number.NEGATIVE_INFINITY / 4;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: k + 1 }, () => [NEG, NEG])
  );
  dp[0][0][0] = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
      // keep i
      dp[i][t][0] = Math.max(dp[i-1][t][0], dp[i-1][t][1]) + A[i-1];
      // remove i
      if (t > 0) dp[i][t][1] = dp[i-1][t-1][0];
    }
  }
  return Math.max(dp[n][k][0], dp[n][k][1]);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How to reduce space to O(k)?
- How to modify for at-most-k removals?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests DP formulation for selecting multiple non-overlapping subarrays with reconstruction—a common pattern in budget allocation and resource optimization scenarios.

## Key Concepts
- **DP over prefixes and block counts**: State `dp[i][t]` tracks the optimal solution using only the first i elements for exactly t blocks.
- **Prefix sums**: Enable O(1) computation of any subarray sum as `pref[r] - pref[l-1]`.
- **Backpointers**: Store decision history to reconstruct the actual subarray boundaries after computing the DP table.
- **Time/space complexity**: Baseline O(n²k) time and O(nk) space; can be optimized with divide-and-conquer or convex hull tricks for larger inputs.

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKSubarrays(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const pref = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) pref[i] = pref[i - 1] + A[i - 1];

  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const choice = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => new Array(k + 1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      choice[i][t] = { type: 'skip', i: i - 1 };

      for (let s = 1; s <= i; s++) {
        const val = dp[s - 1][t - 1] + pref[i] - pref[s - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          choice[i][t] = { type: 'start', s, i };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct boundaries
  const blocks = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (t > 0 && i > 0) {
    const c = choice[i][t];
    if (c.type === 'start') {
      blocks.unshift([c.s, c.i]);
      i = c.s - 1;
    } else {
      i = c.i;
    }
    t--;
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], boundaries: blocks };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design an optimized DP on a circular constraint and track the number of optimal solutions.

## Key Concepts
- DP on linear sequences with spacing (dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[i-d] + v[i]))
- Reducing circularity to two linear cases (include first vs exclude first)
- Counting distinct optimal subsets with tie handling (sum counts when equal)

## Code Example
```javascript
function linearDP(arr, d){
  const n = arr.length; const dp = Array(n+1).fill(0); const ways = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 0; ways[0] = 1;
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
    const take = arr[i-1] + (i-d>=0 ? dp[i-d] : 0);
    const skip = dp[i-1];
    if(take>skip){ dp[i]=take; ways[i]=(i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1); }
    else if(take<skip){ dp[i]=skip; ways[i]=ways[i-1]; }
    else { dp[i]=skip; ways[i] = ((i-d>=0 ? ways[i-d] : 1) + ways[i-1]); }
  }
  return [dp[n], ways[n]];
}

function maxCircular(v, d){
  const [bestA, cntA] = linearDP(v.slice(1), d); // exclude first
  const n = v.length;
  const seg = (n > 2*d-1) ? v.slice(d, n-d+1) : [];
  const [bestBseg, cntBseg] = seg.length ? linearDP(seg, d) : [0,1];
  const bestB = v[0] + bestBseg;
  if(bestA > bestB) return [bestA, cntA];
  if(bestB > bestA) return [bestB, cntBseg];
  return [bestA, cntA + cntBseg];
}
```

